{"question": "A 32-year-old pregnant woman presents to her physician with flu-like symptoms for the past 3 days. She has a runny nose, mild headache, and feels achy. She has a mild fever and diffuse rash over her body. She is concerned about the health of her baby and wants to know if there are any medications that will make her feel better in a short time. Currently, she is at her 29th week of gestation and has received the tetanus vaccination as part of the antenatal care program she received since she immigrated at the end of her first trimester from Romania. She is otherwise healthy. Considering this patient, she is at a higher risk of giving birth to a newborn who will present with which of the following options?", "options": {"A": "Single S2", "B": "Wide pulse pressure", "C": "Brachial-femoral delay", "D": "Double split S2", "E": "Tricuspid valve regurgitation"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Obstetrics", "Infectious Diseases", "Maternal-Fetal Medicine", "Public Health", "Pharmacology"], "option_domains": ["Obstetrics", "Cardiology"], "question_analyses": {"Obstetrics": "In this scenario, the 32-year-old pregnant woman is presenting with flu-like symptoms, including a runny nose, mild headache, body aches, mild fever, and a diffuse rash. Given her recent immigration from Romania and the symptoms described, one potential concern is the risk of a viral infection, particularly one that could affect the fetus. One of the notable infections that could be considered in this context is **rubella** (German measles). Rubella is known to cause mild symptoms in adults but can have serious consequences for the fetus if contracted during pregnancy, especially in the first and second trimesters. The fact that she has received the tetanus vaccination does not provide immunity against rubella, which is typically part of the MMR (measles, mumps, rubella) vaccine. If she was not vaccinated against rubella prior to her pregnancy, she could be at r